== RUNNING
    -backend NAME     interp (default), compile, cranelift,
                      cranelift-generic (backend.overview)
    -warnings MODE    default (print warnings, run), off (no warning
                      pass), error (reject a program that has a warning)
                      (lint.policy)
    -target NAME      the target (operating system and architecture) a
                      .bot program is compiled, or a .hirx file read, for:
                      linux-x86_64, linux-aarch64, macos-x86_64,
                      macos-aarch64, windows-x86_64, windows-x86_64-gnu,
                      windows-aarch64; default the host's
                      (backend.targets)
    -L DIR            add DIR to the system linker's library search path,
                      for a program that calls C (concept.c-ffi)

== PROGRAM ARGUMENTS (argv())
    -argv TEXT        append one argument (its UTF-8 bytes); the first one
                      given is argument zero
    -argv-hex HEX     append one argument given as the hex of its raw bytes
                      (so invalid UTF-8 is expressible; "" is an empty one)
    -argv-none        the empty argument vector
Without any of them argv() is the one-element default ["botlish-runner"],
never the compiler's own arguments. An executable ignores them: it reads its
own process arguments.

== BUILDING (nothing runs)
    -emit-native-executable   a standalone executable beside each input
                              (backend.native-executable)
    -emit-hir-interchange     each input's analyzed HIR as FILE.hirx
                              (backend.hir-interchange)

== CHECKING (nothing runs, nothing is built or written)
    -check            analyze each .bot input and report what compiling it
                      reports: the same error lines (and help lines), its
                      warnings on stderr, "ok" when it has no error; exit
                      status 1 when an input has one. Only -warnings and
                      -target combine with it (they decide the analysis);
                      any other option, or an input that is not a .bot
                      file, is a malformed invocation.

== INSPECTING (printed before the program runs)
    -code             the Tcl code the compile backend generates
    -hir              the program's semantic HIR
    -ast              a .bot file's surface syntax tree
    -aot              every function's closed-AOT readiness
    -aot-data         the same analysis as data
    -aot-spec         the AOT report of every specialized function instance
    -contexts         the context structs, installations and every
                      function's context requirements, with the chain that
                      explains each
    -direct           each module's directness (concept.direct-modules)
    -traits           traits, conformances and specializations
    -emit-nir         the native IR (NIR) of the program
    -emit-clif        the Cranelift IR of every function

== REFERENCE
    -help [QUERY...]  Compilerpedia: alone, its indices; with a query, the
                      topic or the matching topics. Every argument after
                      -help is part of the query. --help and -h are the same.
    -version          the build identity (--version is the same)

== ENVIRONMENT
    BOTLISH_WARNINGS=default|off|error   the warning mode of a compilation
                                         given no -warnings
    BOTLISH_HIR_CACHE=DIR                the front-end cache
    BOTLISH_NATIVE_LINKER=rustc|stub     how executables are linked

== MALFORMED INVOCATIONS
An unknown option, an option without its argument (`-backend` last), an
unknown backend, mode or target, a malformed -argv-hex, -check beside an
option it does not take or with an input that is not Botlish source, or
options without an input file print a message on stderr and exit with
status 2; nothing is compiled.

@topic cli.output
title: What a run prints
summary: Per file: "== FILE (BACKEND)", the program's output (native backends), "   value: V"; or "   error:" lines, each with a "   help:" line naming its topic; warnings go to stderr.
index: backends
alias: output format; value line; what main prints
keywords: value, ==, error, help, warning, stderr, stdout, refinements
related: cli.usage cli.exit-status concept.program-value backend.io lint.policy
docs: README.md

== A RUN
    == game.bot (cranelift)
    ...what the program printed (native backends)...
       value: 42
The value is the program's last expression, shown as a literal ("text",
[1, 2], {a: 1}, enum cases by name). With -backend compile the inferred
types of top-level bindings follow ("   type:  name : T"). A Block value
(a function) is followed by the refinements visible in its environment.

== A REJECTED PROGRAM
    == broken.bot (interp)
       error: broken.bot:3:5: unbound name "x" (CORE SEMANTIC UNBOUND)
       help: botlish -help error.unbound
Every independent diagnostic gets its own line (DIAGNOSTIC-RECOVERY.md);
each is followed by a help line naming the Compilerpedia topic of its rule
or code, once per topic per file.

== WARNINGS
Printed on stderr as they are found, `FILE:LINE:COL: warning: MESSAGE
(CODE)` with `note:` lines for related locations, then one `help:` line per
distinct code. Standard output stays the program's.

== A CHECK
    == game.bot (check)
       ok
With -check (cli.options) the header names no backend and nothing runs: a
program without an error gets "ok"; a rejected one the same "error:" and
"help:" lines a run prints, and its warnings go to stderr as a run's do.

@topic cli.exit-status
title: Exit statuses of the compiler and of executables
summary: botlish exits 0 when every input compiled and ran, 1 when any failed, 2 for a malformed invocation; -help exits 1 when nothing matches; an executable exits 0 or, after a run-time error, nonzero.
index: backends
alias: exit code; exit codes; compiler exit status
keywords: exit, status, 0, 1, 2, failure
related: cli.usage capability.process-exit-status backend.native-executable

== THE COMPILER
    0   every input compiled and ran (or was built, or checked without an
        error), or usage, -help with a match, -version
    1   some input was rejected or failed at run time; or -help found no
        topic (near matches on stderr)
    2   a malformed invocation (stderr says what)

== A STANDALONE EXECUTABLE
0 after printing the program's value; nonzero after a run-time error (on
stderr). The program cannot choose its own exit status
(capability.process-exit-status).
